We prove unconditionally that either NE contains a language requiring Boolean circuits of size at least $2^n/(2n)$, or $VP^0_{\mathbb{Z}}
e VNP^0_{\mathbb{Z}}$, a constant-free version of Valiant's conjecture over the integers. The Boolean alternative implies that $NP
ot\subseteq SIZE[n^k]$ for every constant $k\ge1$. Our proof gives a fine-grained variant of the connection between Polynomial Identity Testing (PIT) and circuit lower bounds established by Kabanets and Impagliazzo (2004). We show that the specific identity tests required by this argument can be performed over the integers without a derandomization assumption.
  Over a fixed finite field $\mathbb{F}$, we first give a deterministic $O(st^{d-1})$-time algorithm for PIT on size-$s$ circuits computing degree-$d$ polynomials in $t$ variables. We further show that, for any fixed $d\ge2$ and $\varepsilon>0$, improving this running time by a factor of $t^{\varepsilon}$, even nondeterministically, implies either the same Boolean lower bound or an arithmetic circuit lower bound of $t^{1+\varepsilon/2}$ for a polynomial family from $VNP_{\mathbb{F}}$. If nondeterministic PIT instead runs in time $s^{o(d)}t^{(1-\varepsilon)d}$ for infinitely many degrees $d$, then the arithmetic lower bound strengthens to $VP_{\mathbb{F}}
e VNP_{\mathbb{F}}$. Over the integers, we give a deterministic PIT algorithm for syntactically set-multilinear circuits running in time $s^{O(1)}t^{5d/6}$, which yields the unconditional result above.
  Combining the Kabanets-Impagliazzo argument with range avoidance, we show that an NP algorithm for PIT over $\mathbb{Z}$ gives a purely arithmetic win-win: either $VP_{\mathbb{Z}}
e VNP_{\mathbb{Z}}$, or constant-degree polynomials of maximum circuit complexity can be constructed in FNP.

For a fixed $k$-uniform hypergraph $F$, let $\mathrm{ex}_k(n,F)$ denote the maximum number of edges in an $F$-free $k$-uniform hypergraph on $n$ vertices. We prove that for every integer $k\ge2$ and every rational $r\in[0,1]$ there exists a $k$-uniform hypergraph $F_{k,r}$ such that
  \begin{equation*}
  \mathrm{ex}_k(n,F_{k,r})=\Theta(n^{k-r}).
  \end{equation*}
  The case $k=2$ is the rational exponents conjecture of Erd\H{o}s and Simonovits \cite{Erdos1988}, also known as Erd\H{o}s problem \#571 \cite{BloomErdoswebsite}; a proof was announced in September 2026 and is expounded by Conlon et al.\ \cite{Conlon-Janzer-Jiang-Kim-Lee-Liu-Longbrake-Yepremyan}. For $k\ge3$ the theorem extends their result to $k$-uniform hypergraphs. In the single-hypergraph setting, this strengthens Fitch's finite-family theorem \cite{Fitch} by realizing each exponent with a single forbidden $k$-uniform hypergraph for $0\le r\le1$. We also show, via an explicit two-edge construction, that for $k\ge4$ this strengthening fails for $\lceil k/2\rceil<r\le k-1$: no single $k$-uniform hypergraph $F$ then satisfies $\mathrm{ex}_k(n,F)=\Theta(n^{k-r})$. The main result of this paper has been formalized in Lean 4, conditional on two explicitly stated axioms.

Let F be a saturated fusion system on a finite 2-group S, and let B(F) be the Burnside ring of F. Barsotti and Carman proved that when S is abelian, B(F)^*=B(S)^* if and only if F=F_S(S). Barsotti and Carman asked on page 728 of their paper whether there exists a counterexample to Corollary 9.3 for a nonabelian 2-group. We have given a positive answer. Our main conclusion is that the following are equivalent for all finite 2-groups:
  (1)F=F_S(S);
  (2)B(F)^*=B(S)^*;
  (3)For every maximal subgroup M of S, 1-[S/M] is in B(F)^*.

For an \(n\)-vertex graph \(G\) and an integer \(m\ge n\), let \(f'(G,m)\) be the minimum number of distinct color sets used by proper colorings from \(m\)-element lists that are disjoint at distinct nonadjacent vertices. A clique-partition assignment gives a common list to the vertices of each part of a clique partition and uses disjoint lists on different parts. In this paper, we construct a graph on \(20\) vertices for which these assignments do not attain \(f'(G,20)\), and a fixed graph on \(25\) vertices for which they fail to attain the minimum for every allowed list size. For every triangle-free graph \(G\) on \(n\ge2\) vertices, we prove \[
  f'(G,m)= inom{m}{2}^{
u(G)}m^{n-2
u(G)}
  \qquad (m\ge n), \] where \(
u(G)\) is the size of a maximum matching, and characterize all minimizing assignments in terms of maximum matchings. For clique blow-ups of each fixed odd cycle of length at least five, we determine the minimum up to constant factors uniformly over the blow-up size and all allowed list sizes, and obtain an exponential separation from the clique-partition value. We also prove that a noncomplete graph \(G\) satisfies \(f'(G,n)=n^2\) if and only if all edges of its complement have a common endpoint. These results disprove the general clique-partition conjecture of Gravier, Maffray, and Mohar (\emph{Discrete Mathematics}, 2003) and its extension by Wu and Zhang (\emph{Applied Mathematics Letters}, 2006), while establishing both triangle-free conjectures.
